# SYNOPSIS

```usage
nl [-p] [-b type] [-d delim] [-f type] [-h type] [-i num] [-l num]
   [-n format] [-s sep] [-v num] [-w num] [file]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`nl` copies <file>, or standard input when there is no <file> or it is `-`, to standard output, numbering lines.
A numbered line is preceded by its number, right-aligned in 6 columns, and a tab.
It is sbase's command, in `/boot/bin/posix`: the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) binds that directory before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it there.

The input is read as logical pages, each a header, a body and a footer.
A line holding only the delimiter three times starts a header, twice a body, and once a footer; the delimiter is `\:`, and the line itself is not copied.
Input before any such line is body.
Numbering starts again at each logical page.

: `-b` <type>
  How to number body lines; the default is `t`.
  The types are `a`, every line; `t`, every non-empty line; `n`, none; and `p`<expr>, the lines that match the basic regular expression <expr>.
: `-d` <delim>
  Use <delim> as the delimiter; a single character is followed by `:`.
: `-f` <type>
  How to number footer lines; the default is `n`.
: `-h` <type>
  How to number header lines; the default is `n`.
: `-i` <num>
  Add <num> to the number for each numbered line, instead of 1.
: `-l` <num>
  With type `a`, count <num> empty lines in a row as one, numbering only the last of each group.
: `-n` <format>
  Write numbers right-aligned (`rn`, the default), left-aligned (`ln`), or right-aligned with leading zeros (`rz`).
: `-p`
  Do not start numbering again at logical pages.
: `-s` <sep>
  Write <sep>, in which backslash escapes such as `\t` are understood, after each number instead of a tab.
: `-v` <num>
  Start each logical page at <num>, instead of 1.
: `-w` <num>
  Write numbers <num> columns wide, instead of 6.

